WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.217
When two measured variables change together,
their data can show a correlation.

2
00:00:05.217 --> 00:00:10.575
A positive relationship means they tend to
rise together; a negative relationship means

3
00:00:10.575 --> 00:00:13.872
one tends to fall as the other rises.

4
00:00:13.872 --> 00:00:16.182
Correlation describes a pattern.

5
00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:19.415
It does not yet explain why the pattern
exists.

6
00:00:19.538 --> 00:00:24.080
Ice-cream sales and reported crime can both
rise during hot weather.

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.436
That does not mean buying ice cream causes
crime.

8
00:00:27.436 --> 00:00:32.218
Temperature changes behavior in ways that can
affect both measurements.

9
00:00:32.218 --> 00:00:37.426
This third-variable problem is one reason an
observed relationship cannot establish a

10
00:00:37.426 --> 00:00:39.596
causal direction on its own.

11
00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:45.956
To support a causal claim, researchers need a
design that manipulates an independent

12
00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:51.230
variable and controls competing explanations,
often with comparison groups and random

13
00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:51.710
assignment.

14
00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:56.886
Observational correlations remain valuable for
prediction and discovery.

15
00:00:56.886 --> 00:01:02.144
The professional habit is to make a claim no
stronger than the study design allows.
